"Score each option 1-5 on each criterion. There is no NA: a field that says \"none\" or \"none known\" scores 1. Give each score a reason of at most 20 words. Score from the quantities, sources and gaps the option names; ignore verdict words such as COMPUTE-BOUND or NOT COMPUTE-BOUND. Also label each option SAME, ADJACENT or ELSEWHERE against the root question, with a reason: SAME if it asks the root question or a sub-question of it, ADJACENT if it has the root's subject but asks a different question, ELSEWHERE if its subject is different. Decide the subject from the title and summary; count root_link only if the summary itself describes the root question's object of study. Do not add up the scores. Decide only from the text below; do not use tools.
Root question: Can molecular mapping of AAV capsid-receptor interactions on brain microvascular endothelial cells determine which AAV variants transcytose across the blood-brain barrier versus remain intracellular, and for which capsid-receptor binding signatures?
Criteria:
stakes: Suppose the question this option raises were answered, either way. 5 = a choice someone faces (what to buy, build, collect or scale next) turns on the answer, and either answer would move it; 3 = the answer would refine a belief but no choice turns on it, or one answer is already much more likely than the other; 1 = either answer is already expected, or nothing would change. Do not score how famous or important the field is, or how close it is to the root question.
open: Has published work already measured how the answer changes with the quantity compute buys here (samples, ensemble members, resolution, model runs, model size, search width), across the range that $1,000 of Lambda on-demand GPU time (for scale, about 250 H100-hours) can reach? 5 = names the quantity and where published measurements stop (or that none exist), and the unmeasured range starts within that reach; 3 = the unmeasured range is reachable only through a smaller stand-in system, or published work already covers part of the reachable range; 1 = names no compute-bought quantity, or published work already covers everything within that reach, so the open range lies only beyond it.
reshape: Does the option name a part of the work that one answer waits on step after step, and a reason those steps might not have to run in order? 5 = names that chain and roughly how long it is; one answer leaves most of a GPU idle (a small state, or few runs needed, so copies side by side would not fill it); names what would have to be true of the system for the steps to be computed at the same time, in a form a cheap measurement could check; and no cited source already computes this chain in parallel for this system; 3 = names the chain and a way to restructure it, but one of these fails: the answer needs many independent runs, so copies side by side already fill the hardware; one run already fills a GPU; or no checkable condition is named; 1 = sequential says none, or the only parallelism is independent pieces (copies, a sweep, one job per GPU), or a cited source already computes this chain in parallel for this system. A method published for a different system does not lower this score. Do not score how famous the method or field is.
Options, one per line:
{\"id\":\"O\",\"title\":\"Structural coverage of capsid–receptor–glycan complexes\",\"summary\":\"Exhaustive AlphaFold-Multimer, cryo-ET and MD modelling of capsid–receptor–glycan complexes across many variants, receptors and pH values would turn epitope geometry and pH-resolved kinetics from a per-complex experiment into a computed prior (method precedent: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11381518).\",\"root_link\":\"Bears on the \\"for which capsid–receptor binding signatures\\" half of the question — whether the epitope-geometry and pH-resolved-kinetics components of a signature can be computed instead of measured per complex.\",\"compute_open\":\"Model runs (capsid–receptor–glycan complexes predicted per pH condition): the largest amount a cited source reports is AlphaFold-Multimer runs of 5 structural models per capsid peptide with up to 20 recycles, paired with cryo-ET sub-tomogram averaging of 85,135 symmetry-expanded particles (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11381518), and what is still unknown above that amount is whether any scale of computed coverage recovers epitope geometry and pH-resolved off-rates accurately enough to classify transcytosis versus retention, since the only quantitative pH-resolved benchmark in hand is a single measured pair (ALPL–VCAP-102, K_D ≈ 20 nM at pH 7.4 with rapid dissociation at pH 5.5: https://www.cell.com/molecular-therapy/fulltext/S1525-0016(25)00373-9).\",\"sequential\":\"One run is a chain of roughly 10^9–10^12 integration steps at about 2 fs each, every step waiting on the previous coordinates, and a full answer needs several independent replicas per (capsid, receptor, pH) condition, with one replica keeping a GPU-resident MD engine busy for its whole length (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9281402/). The steps must run in order inside a single trajectory, but the answer can be assembled instead from many short independent trajectories started at the same time, which pays off when metastable states separate timescales so replicas decorrelate and weak scaling of independent runs beats strong scaling of one run (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9476681/); the nearest source that tried it is adaptive-sampling Markov state modelling, where wall-clock efficiency scaled linearly up to 5,000 parallel simulations per iteration (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3637129/).\"}
{\"id\":\"V1\",\"title\":\"Full glycosylated capsid–receptor–glycan complexes instead of peptide fragments\",\"summary\":\"Model full 60-mer AAV capsids against membrane-anchored, glycosylated receptors — GPI-anchored ALPL, glycosylated LRP6 and CA-IV — with glycans included, using AlphaFold-Multimer for receptor domains, cryo-ET for the assembled complex and MD for pH-resolved contacts, rather than peptide–receptor-domain fragments in water. This would turn epitope geometry and pH-resolved contact kinetics into a computed prior for the actual glycosylated capsid–receptor–glycan complexes that Voyager's ALPL-NeuroShuttle and the Gradinaru lab's LRP6 mapping programme study. The root question still asks for which capsid–receptor binding signatures, but the signature now includes glycan and membrane context instead of only peptide-domain contacts. If the full-complex prior recovers receptor selectivity and pH-dependent release, it would give those programmes a structural rationale for receptor choice before Transwell or in vivo work.\",\"root_link\":\"Bears on the \\"for which capsid–receptor binding signatures\\" half by asking whether epitope geometry and pH-resolved kinetics computed on the glycosylated, membrane-anchored complexes match the signatures the programmes actually select on.\",\"compute_open\":\"Full 60-mer capsid–receptor–glycan systems are far larger than AlphaFold-Multimer runs of 5 models per capsid peptide and cryo-ET averaging of 85,135 symmetry-expanded particles, and it is unknown whether any affordable scale of computed coverage recovers glycan-dependent receptor selectivity; the only quantitative pH-resolved benchmark remains the single measured ALPL–VCAP-102 pair.\",\"sequential\":\"The MD trajectory is still a chain of roughly 10^9–10^12 integration steps at about 2 fs each, each waiting on the previous coordinates, and full glycosylated 60-mer systems make each step more expensive; the answer can still be assembled from many independent replicas per (capsid, receptor, pH) condition, as in adaptive-sampling Markov state modelling that scaled linearly to 5,000 parallel simulations per iteration.\"}
{\"id\":\"V2\",\"title\":\"Cell-level Transwell transcytosis as the comparison\",\"summary\":\"Keep the AlphaFold-Multimer and pH-resolved MD calculations, but judge the computed signature against cell-level transcytosis — Transwell flux across human brain microvascular endothelial cells and receptor-knockout or knockdown blockade — for the same capsid–receptor pairs, rather than against its own 10-ns contact-survival labels or a 320-ns sequential run. The classifier would be calibrated to the endpoint Voyager's TRACER platform and the Gradinaru lab use when deciding whether a capsid crosses the BBB or stays in endothelium. A computed AUC would then mean separation of transcytosing from retained capsids in the assay those programmes trust, not separation of self-defined contact classes. If it fails, the negative result would also be interpretable against their decision endpoint rather than only against a proxy.\",\"root_link\":\"Shifts the link from the computed signature alone to whether that signature predicts the cell-level transcytosis versus endothelial-retention outcome the programmes measure.\",\"compute_open\":\"The structural and MD runs are unchanged, but the open quantity is now whether any computed signature correlates with Transwell flux and receptor blockade across multiple capsid–receptor pairs; no programme has published such a computed classifier, and the only measured pH-kinetics anchor is still ALPL–VCAP-102.\",\"sequential\":\"Unchanged: one trajectory is a chain of roughly 10^9–10^12 ordered 2-fs steps, but the answer can be assembled from many short independent replicas per (capsid, receptor, pH) condition, with weak scaling of replicas as in adaptive-sampling Markov state modelling up to 5,000 parallel simulations per iteration.\"}
{\"id\":\"V3\",\"title\":\"Millisecond pH-resolved off-rates and release probabilities, not ten-ns contact survival\",\"summary\":\"Keep the peptide–receptor fragment system and AlphaFold-Multimer docking, but spend the extra compute on pH-resolved off-rates and endosomal-release probabilities estimated from many short replicas with adaptive-sampling Markov state models, rather than on ten-ns contact-survival proxies. Scale to roughly 5,000 parallel simulations per iteration, matching the adaptive-sampling precedent, and require error bars on the pH ratio for each capsid–receptor pair. The quantity bought is then a kinetic signature closer to the millisecond-scale off-rates and endosomal release steps that determine transcytosis, which is what Voyager, Dyno and the Gradinaru lab need to distinguish transport from retention. If the pH ratio does not separate at that scale, the negative result would rule out the kinetic-signature mechanism more strongly than the original ten-ns proxy could.\",\"root_link\":\"Bears on the \\"for which capsid–receptor binding signatures\\" half by making pH-resolved kinetics a computed quantity with error bars rather than a ten-ns survival proxy.\",\"compute_open\":\"The cited adaptive-sampling precedent reached linear wall-clock efficiency up to 5,000 parallel simulations per iteration, and the largest structural coverage cited is 5 AlphaFold-Multimer models per peptide with cryo-ET of 85,135 particles; what remains unknown is whether 5,000 short replicas per condition can estimate millisecond-scale pH-resolved off-rates accurately enough to classify transcytosis versus retention, since only ALPL–VCAP-102 has measured pH kinetics.\",\"sequential\":\"One trajectory is still 10^9–10^12 ordered 2-fs steps, but the answer is assembled from many independent short trajectories; at 5,000 parallel simulations per iteration the weak scaling of independent replicas is the lever, as in adaptive-sampling Markov state modelling.\"}

